From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Your Name <you@example.org>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] ALSA: hda/realtek: Fix speaker output on ASUS Vivobook Pro 14X M7400QC

The ASUS Vivobook Pro 14X OLED M7400QC (PCI SSID 1043:1ab2, Realtek
ALC294, single internal speaker on NID 0x14, no external smart amp)
has no matching entry in the quirk table, so no fixup is applied.

After a warm reboot from Windows the speaker output is silent or
crackles, and only a full power-off brings it back, so the bad state
lives in the codec and survives a warm reboot.  Dumping the Realtek
COEF registers on NID 0x20 after a cold boot and after a warm reboot
from Windows (BIOS M7400QC.311) gives:

  COEF    cold boot    warm reboot from Windows
  0x0f    0x____       0x____
  0x1b    0x____       0x____
  0x40    0x____       0x____

[Describe the measured difference here.  This fixup assumes it is
bit 10 of COEF 0x1b, the bit the Windows driver leaves cleared on the
ASUS UX431DA (ALC294_FIXUP_ASUS_COEF_1B, bug 207553) and on the Intel
Hades Canyon ALC700.]

Restore only bit 10 of COEF 0x1b at every init.  A read-modify-write
keeps the other bits at the value this codec came up with; writing a
whole value such as 0x4e4b was reported to break cold boot on another
ALC294 model (bug 219415).  Chain ALC294_FIXUP_ASUS_HPE for the EAPD
COEF 0x0f and the ASUS headset-mic pin, as the other ALC294
Vivobook/Zenbook entries do.

With the fixup, COEF 0x1b reads 0x____ after a warm reboot from
Windows, and the speaker works after cold boot, warm reboot from
Windows, suspend/resume and runtime suspend.

Link: https://bugzilla.kernel.org/show_bug.cgi?id=207553
Link: https://bugzilla.kernel.org/show_bug.cgi?id=219415
Link: https://askubuntu.com/questions/1420976
Cc: <stable@vger.kernel.org>
Signed-off-by: Your Name <you@example.org>
---
